s</italic> miRNAs are 5&#x02032; related to 98 human miRNAs (<xref ref-type="supplementary-material" rid="pone.0002818.s014">Dataset S10</xref>), and 22 nematode miRNAs are &#x02265;70&#x00025; identical over the full length of 46 human miRNAs (<xref ref-type="supplementary-material" rid="pone.0002818.s015">Dataset S11</xref>). Worm-human miRNAs with weaker identity (60&#x02013;69.9&#x00025;) over full sequence are detailed in <xref ref-type="supplementary-material" rid="pone.0002818.s016">Dataset S12</xref>. Group IDs correspond to <italic>C. elegans</italic> miRNAs with human related-sequences.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="pone-0002818-t005" position="float"><object-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1371/journal.pone.0002818.t005</object-id><label>Table 5</label><caption><title>Analysis of 5&#x02032; and &#x02265;70&#x00025; similarity groups identifies 83 <italic>D. melanogaster</italic>-human miRNA families including 83 fly miRNAs and 121 human miRNAs.</title></caption><graphic id="pone-0002818-t005-5" xlink:href="pone.0002818.t005"/><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" alternate-form-of="pone-0002818-t005-5"><colgroup span="1"><col align="left" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/><col align="center" span="1"/></colgroup><thead><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">miRNA Group ID</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>D.melanogaster</italic> miRNA</td><td colspan="2" align="left" rowspan="1">Human Sequence-Related miRNAs</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">5&#x02032; Sequence</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Full Sequence</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">bantam</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">dme-bantam</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">hsa-miR-450b-3p</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/></tr><tr><td align="left